00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.300
So let's take a look
at the day in the life

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:52.050
of a capacity admin
in Microsoft Fabric.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.270
SPEAKER 1: It's December,
year-end close.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.670
Every query matters, and
somewhere in marketing,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.890
someone just kicked
off a monster report

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.600
across three years of data.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.000
The CFO's dashboard
is about to crawl.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.140
Guess who gets the call?

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.200
So I open the "Admin portal,"
head to the "Capacity settings,"

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.510
and click into my
production capacity.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:10.860
First layer of defense:

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:12.960
workspace level
"Surge Protection."

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.360
I'm capping every ad
hoc reporting workspace

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.730
at 25% of the capacity.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.560
Marketing can still
run their reports;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.420
they just can't eat
the whole buffet.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.030
I also flip on capacity
level surge protection,

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.730
which rejects background
operations above the threshold,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.100
keeping room for the
interactive reports

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.770
that actually matter right now.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.440
But the sales workspaces,
those are different.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.180
I marked them as
mission-critical.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.190
That means they're exempt
from the surge cap.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.350
They get whatever
capacity they need

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.500
because year-end close
doesn't wait for anyone.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.300
Now I need a safety valve.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.650
I enable capacity overage and
set a 24-hour rolling CU limit.

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:52.590
If sales needs to spike beyond
our capacity during crunch time,

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.740
it can -- no throttling,
no angry CFO.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.290
But protection without
visibility is just a hope.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.940
So I open the "Real-Time" Hub,

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.270
select capacity overview
events and set an alert.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.630
I name the rule pick summary
events, and I add a filter

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.140
when the interactive
delay threshold hits 90%.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.430
Fire a Teams message.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:11.120
That's it.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.250
A few clicks, and I've
got real-time eyes

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.680
on my capacity before
the dashboard spins,

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.940
before the phone rings,

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.380
before anyone even knows
there's a problem.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.100
Four layers: noisy neighbors
cap, crown jewels protected,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.100
overflow valve set, real time
eyes on the whole thing.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:29.730
The CFO's dashboards,
it never even blinked.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:32.100
KIM MANIS: There's a ton of
new capabilities when it comes

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.430
to capacity administration
and how you can govern both

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.970
with proactive guardrails
