You don't get in.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.050
Simple, decisive.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.380
Inside the workspace roles
define what you can do.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:11:59.730
Megan is a contributor.

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.890
She can create and delete items.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.360
David is a viewer.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.200
Read only.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.560
The right access for the right
person at the right level.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.630
Now I could go further.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:13.530
"Inbound networking" lets me
block public access entirely

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:16.560
and restrict connections
to our corporate IP range

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.720
through the firewall,
but honestly,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.860
Conditional Access plus
workspace roles already gives me

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:25.320
a strong balance of
security and usability,

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.960
so I'll keep the default
inbound access for now.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.270
Where it gets
serious is outbound.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.580
I enable outbound
access protection,

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.120
which blocks every outgoing
connection from this workspace.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.400
Nothing leaves unless I say so.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:40.530
Then I open the doors I trust.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.440
I connect the rule for our
sales aggregation pipeline

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.250
to reach order data in Azure
and a managed private endpoint

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.760
for a partner storage account
with currency conversion rates.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.610
This is active only after
the storage account owner

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:54.570
approves it.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:55.650
Now, watch.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.270
A user tries to
connect the notebook

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:58.980
to a personal storage account,

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.740
maybe copying some data
somewhere it shouldn't go.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:05.970
Fabric blocks it, no warning,
no negotiation, just no.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:09.450
Conditional Access, workspace
rows, outbound protection.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.930
Three layers: identity,
permissions, and network.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.090
And the data never
leaves the building.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:18.480
That's not a policy
document; that's a platform.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.430
KIM MANIS: We have all
the capabilities you need

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.670
to secure your data estate
with Microsoft Fabric.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.190
At the tenant level, you
can use Microsoft Entra

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.850
to authenticate every inbound
request using all available data

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.530
points like identity, location,
device, and risk detection.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:37.890
Or for specific scenarios, you
can use Azure Private Link

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:41.520
to send traffic over Microsoft's
own private network,

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.980
and of course,
everything is encrypted

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.680
and secured by
default in Fabric.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:48.240
However, we've heard
from customers

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.020
that you need more granular
control at the workspace level,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.780
project by project, to make
sure you're securing your data

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.630
appropriately for
the project at hand,

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:01.110
and that's why we have workspace
level security for all

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:02.370
of the capabilities you need,

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.190
whether it's outbound
trusted workspace access,

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.860
Azure Private Link,
outbound access protection,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.420
customer managed keys,

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:12.000
workspace-level IP
firewall rules.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.460
All of these capabilities
are generally available

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.250
and allow you to give
the granularity you need

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.410
and the security levels
you need for each

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.900
of the different projects
going on in Fabric.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.510
Okay, so we already talked
about Fabric tenant admins

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:26.640
and capacity and
workspace admins.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.150
Now let's switch gears and talk
about data and AI developers

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.770
and how they can take advantage

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.960
of the governance
capabilities in Fabric.

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:40.260
And automating developer
workflows are critical

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.790
for governance.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.180
You need to make sure you
reduce manual configurations,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.760
and you don't want humans
in the loop creating issues

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.290
that you could automate.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:52.110
And you need visibility
into what is going

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.720
on to make sure you
can audit everything,

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.200
and inconsistent processes
are really hard to enforce,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.270
and again, this is
where automation

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:03.660
around those developer workloads
really help you make sure

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.290
that every project is
following best practices.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.530
And Fabric's promise to
developers are simple.

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.930
We rely on consistent
open formats,

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.780
making sure your
data is your data.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:19.740
Developers need access
to scriptable APIs.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:21.930
They need automation
for provisioning

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:23.880
through tools like Terraform.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:26.400
We need to make
collaboration simple

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.160
through Git-based source
control, and then, of course,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.920
whether you're
developing in the UI

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:35.700
through low-friction navigation
and built-in experiences,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.940
or through agents in
your AI tool of choice,

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.150
we need to make sure that
agentic development is simple

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.920
and obvious with
Microsoft Fabric.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.810
So now let's take a look
at the day in the life

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:53.520
of the data developer setting
up a new project in Fabric.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.680
SPEAKER 2: Let's provision
a complete Fabric Analytics

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:58.870
project with a
single pipeline run.

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.270
Everything's parameterized:
workspace name,

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.480
capacity, auth method, storage.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.340
Configure what you
need and "Run."

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:15.000
The pipeline starts
with a Terraform plan,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.110
cleaning up prior state

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:19.470
and showing exactly
what it will create.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.360
It pauses for manual approval.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.000
You review the full plan
before anything gets created

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.690
and then "Resume."

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.300
The Terraform Fabric provider
creates 12 resources:

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.540
workspaces, identity,
storage connections,

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.130
Git sync -- all as code.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:41.100
Now the Fabric CLI steps
in, patching notebooks,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.920
building the Direct Lake model,
and publishing the environment.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.260
Copy jobs pull data from ADLS
into the Bronze Lakehouse.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:53.970
Notebooks transform
it to Silver,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.790
and shortcuts link the
tables across lakehouses --

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.160
medallion architecture
fully automated.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:00.990
And now the pipeline is done.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:02.890
Let's see the results.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:10.440
Eleven items, the full stack:
lakehouses to reports.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:13.200
Nothing created manually.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.490
Here's the full lineage:

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:17.070
ingestion to transformation
to reporting.

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:19.800
The semantic model connects to
Silver through Direct Lake,

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:23.350
querying the lakehouse
directly, no import step.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:30.690
And in the Silver Lakehouse,
tables and data ready to query.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:35.040
Infrastructure, data, analytics
-- all from one pipeline run.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:36.270
That's the whole story.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.630
Full automation with Fabric.

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.120
KIM MANIS: Developers have a lot
to deal with, and in Fabric,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:45.030
we really want to make it
simple, whether it's how you set

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.040
up the project through
automation with tools

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.260
like Terraform, the developer
workflow, just making it easier

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.810
to jump from dev to test to prod

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:56.220
to collaborate with
your coworkers.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:59.310
And when you deploy to
production, making it simple

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.500
to automate that deployment

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:05.000
so that you can reliably
deploy quality code.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.610
And then, of course, monitoring
and operating that code

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:09.980
in production so that you
know what's going on,

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.780
and you can monitor
everything in the system.

